{"name":"Soba","hex":"#d1b49f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soba","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Japanese buckwheat noodle."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A traditional community leader in Angola."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soba"}],"entries":[{"title":"Soba","blocks":[{"text":"Soba are Japanese noodles made from buckwheat flour, with varying amounts of wheat flour mixed in. \nIt has an ashen brown color, and a slightly grainy texture. The noodles are served either chilled with a dipping sauce, or hot in a noodle soup. They are used in a wide variety of dishes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Dried_soba_noodles_by_FotoosVanRobin.jpg/330px-Dried_soba_noodles_by_FotoosVanRobin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soba"},{"title":"Soba (city)","blocks":[{"text":"Soba is an archaeological site and former town in what is now central Sudan. It was the capital of the medieval Nubian kingdom of Alodia from the sixth century until around 1500. E. A. Wallis Budge identified it with a group of ruins on the Blue Nile 19 kilometres (12 mi) from Khartoum, where there are remains of a Meroitic temple that had been converted into a Christian church."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Christian_Nubia.png/330px-Christian_Nubia.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soba_(city)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Mori_soba_of_Fuji-soba.jpg/500px-Mori_soba_of_Fuji-soba.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Mori_soba_of_Fuji-soba.jpg/1280px-Mori_soba_of_Fuji-soba.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Mori_soba_of_Fuji-soba.jpg/500px-Mori_soba_of_Fuji-soba.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mori soba, served cold with dipping sauce and negi","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMori_soba_of_Fuji-soba.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Udon_soba_introduction_stele.jpg/500px-Udon_soba_introduction_stele.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Udon_soba_introduction_stele.jpg/1280px-Udon_soba_introduction_stele.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Udon_soba_introduction_stele.jpg/500px-Udon_soba_introduction_stele.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stele commemorating the introduction of soba and udon noodles at Jōten-ji temple. Enni, the temple's founder, is traditionally believed to have brought noodlemaking back to Japan from China.[9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUdon_soba_introduction_stele.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Sunaba_izumiya.jpg/500px-Sunaba_izumiya.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Sunaba_izumiya.jpg/1280px-Sunaba_izumiya.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Sunaba_izumiya.jpg/500px-Sunaba_izumiya.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Print depicting Sunaba, a popular soba restaurant from Osaka. By Takehara Shunchōsai, 1796–1798","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASunaba_izumiya.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Machikado-ya_T